ELI5 = {
"c4450c8d": "Work items can name a preferred owner role, and today that soft routing hint can read like the item is actually staffed. This splits the two: a hint stays a hint, and a real assignment must immediately carry staffing, a scheduled continuation, a typed block, or a terminal state, so nothing sits in a limbo that looks owned.",
"7f068d0c": "Agents are disposable; roles are the durable offices, and accountability lives with the office. But a card opened against a session key never writes down which role that session was wearing, like a ticket filed against a badge number with no job title. A third of all cards are like that today, so any question asked by role silently misses them. The fix is one stamp at open time.",
"032f11a6": "Every completion currently demands a review, which forces coordinators, who produce no artifact, to invent something reviewable (this exact rule jammed the relief owner's spawn card for a day). Make output kind an archetype property: coordinators complete without artifacts, producers hand over reviewable artifacts, reviewers file verdicts without review-of-review.",
"f6b4b8ad": "A blocked worker has no lawful way to hand the problem up; it sits or surrenders. This defines the standard handoff: the worker states what blocks it and the parent decides what happens next.",
"236e5efa": "When a child assignment ends (done, failed, surrendered) its parent is not reliably woken, so finished work strands below an owner who never hears about it. A terminal child must durably reactivate its open parent.",
"ecd8cd9d": "Surrender today deletes custody: the worker walks away and the work has no holder. Replace it with a typed cannot-proceed state that keeps the assignment, pauses the prodding, and routes the decision to the parent.",
"174a2b8b": "When the session holding work retires or dies, the work just loses staff and goes quiet. Route it upward immediately so unfinished work always has an accountable holder.",
"809821f8": "When a child completes, the parent should be required to choose what happens next (retain, park, or retire) instead of the completion evaporating. This adds that required-disposition rail.",
"3d6d13a0": "Some terminal assignments have no living parent capable of taking the disposition, so the escalation has nowhere to go. Make Main the fallback: when no capable owner remains, terminal work escalates to the root session rather than stranding.",
"89087a49": "Decision requests addressed to an owner can vanish before being ruled; one card had its request disappear and get re-issued about 37 times in two days. A decision request must survive until it is actually ruled.",
"fb8316cf": "Assignments can carry declared file lists, but work-item-get does not show them, so a worker cannot see its own declared scope without raw DB access. Surface the declarations in the trace.",
"888d06d4": "Ruled: assignment file lists are advisory suggestions, not permissions. The substrate must never refuse an edit or an overlapping assignment because a path is listed elsewhere; the lists become visible discovery metadata only.",
"113d569f": "If the turn delivering a wake fails, the wake and the instruction inside it silently evaporate; four briefs were lost this way in one day. Wakes get a delivered-or-failed terminal state, one re-arm, and a visible undelivered bounce to the sender.",
"c01e8f20": "Sending a wake to a session that cannot run (vendor weekly limit, dead) currently succeeds and then dies inside a failed turn; two operator instructions were lost exactly this way. The send itself must fail visibly when the target cannot run.",
"0abae0fd": "Wakes get canceled with no record of who, why, or what replaces them, which can quietly strand open work. Every cancellation records a typed reason, requester, causal source, and disposition.",
"2ef3d514": "Agents poll on timers to notice things like a review landing. Condition wakes let them subscribe to a recorded event and get woken when it happens, with a timeout fallback.",
"7efb0887": "When the harness process fails to submit or execute at all, the owner gets nothing useful back. Return typed process-submission and execution failures to the owning reader instead of a generic dead turn.",
"55b9755b": "When a turn dies for a known safe reason (rate limit, auth, host down) that cause should persist on the assignment, not vanish into adapter logs, so owners act on facts instead of re-diagnosing.",
"6937890c": "There is no lever to stop, park, relaunch, or recycle a running harness process; even retire does not halt turns. Four recovery features block on this one missing primitive, and the nineteen-hour stuck turn of 2026-08-20 is what its absence looks like.",
"cbb56e71": "When an adapter dies and comes back, the assignments its sessions held do not resume; they wait for manual wakes. Make resumption automatic after recovery.",
"6435c199": "You cannot move a live session to a different model or harness without destroying and respawning it, losing role, work, and graph position. Add a supported CLI operation that swaps the engine under a running session.",
"fada1e9a": "What a session falls back to when its model runs out (rate limit, retirement) is ad hoc today. Specify a structured rundown policy and token-gated overrides for spawn-time model choices.",
"d018d4ed": "A wake sent to a dead or retired role bounces back to Main labeled as from user:mike, agent text wearing the human's identity. The bounce must carry truthful attribution and a failure state.",
"d8363deb": "Codex turns repeatedly end as interrupted-outcome-unknown, so nobody can tell whether the work happened. Every turn must end in a truthful terminal outcome.",
"5f670eed": "A re-dispatch reminder wake has no idempotency guard: it fires after the work was already done inline, and obeying it literally duplicates the dispatch. Guard it.",
"24028d10": "A fresh install with only codex onboarded still defaults Main to a Claude model, so every turn fails and the error pushes you to onboard a vendor you do not use. First boot must derive its harness and model from what is onboarded and runnable.",
"e932ed29": "The 50-session cap plus spawner-only retire authority makes orphaned idle sessions (spawner gone) unreclaimable, and they eat capacity; product owners were blocked on routine hires while ~14 idle sessions sat unkillable. Reap authority follows accountability, not dead spawners.",
"c49c1e4a": "There is no generic, accountable way to wind down an idle worker; every end-of-life is improvised. Define the standard disposition.",
"a78f6a03": "Sessions cannot be moved under a new parent when topology needs correcting. Add a bounded re-parent that fixes the current parent without rewriting immutable origin or losing live work.",
"4597c473": "Custody today has one shape, keep the bytes, which is right for a document and wrong for code: a merged worktree should not be hoarded forever. Deliverables get a kind, and the kind decides custody and cleanup.",
"472aa5d8": "Retiring a session can delete its worktree regardless of git state. Retirement must check pushed, merged, and clean first, the enforcement half of the dirty-worktree data-loss bug.",
"a9a31ae2": "When the gateway protocol moves ahead of a satellite's CLI, that CLI is refused for everything, including reporting the problem. Recover it at the codec seam instead of bricking the satellite.",
"56bee7ce": "The full satellite recovery story: on incompatible contact, deliver a verified replacement CLI, swap it atomically, retry the original request, and leave a durable, retryable failure if that does not work.",
"9cb71f52": "A gateway upgrade orphaned eezo's older CLI: agents ran turns fine but could not file anything, and the tool needed to report the refusal was the refused tool. It cost four wasted dispatches and a false diagnosis; upgrades must not strand satellites.",
"b83e1fed": "The credential vault infers health from storage state, and it lied during the gibson spawn-refusal incident. Ruled design: credentials live only in the harness home, a missing file IS the needs-onboarding signal, and expiry is observed from real 401s, never inferred.",
"e86ddd54": "Running tightbeam onboard parks the claude runtime, including the session conducting the onboarding, killing the ceremony mid-flight while the OAuth URL survives in a process nothing can reach. Onboarding must preserve the runtime driving it.",
"ef8401df": "Rows filed through the HTTP facade are second-class: the stall detector cannot see facade-filed attests (so it nags workers who did file), and facade self-wakes 404, leaving satellite agents with no self-continuation.",
"f1013180": "When the gateway is down, the gateway is also the only channel for reporting that it is down. Add a sanctioned out-of-band path: a disk drop-box ingested on recovery plus an operator-visible beacon.",
"e2d94af7": "Eezo's CLI rejects its own configured ws:// scheme (Unknown Scheme: ws), so every filing from that host is blocked. A client-side transport fix.",
"1e8158f4": "The catalog offers models a host cannot actually route (claude-sonnet-5 offered while gibson placement refused it), so a choice fails after selection. Catalog and placement must agree.",
"038d91b4": "A gateway release can complete while an offline satellite never got its client update, silently orphaning it. Record per-satellite update outcome and offline-pending state in the release record.",
"a57dee58": "Supervision watches a side table of entitlement rows instead of the truth: if that row is missing or wrong, an open card silently leaves the prodded population. Derive who gets watched from open assignments themselves.",
"5870f52e": "Filed progress currently just stretches the backoff, and a card can drift out of the prod-turn loop entirely. Every open live assignment stays in the loop until completion, explicit continuation, or parent elevation.",
"e27eb1a6": "The prodder can exhaust its retry tiers against a failing session and then simply stop, with no elevation and no durable record that it gave up. Exhaustion must route upward with provenance.",
"2a596659": "The effort patrol fires every 15-20 minutes at legitimately parked cards, forcing filing churn that defeats the park (the relief owner's night of two dozen check-in generations). Add a sanctioned hold the patrol respects.",
"0552815b": "Standing ownership cards can never file done, yet the stall detector measures them like finite tasks; one was prodded 26 times during an outage. Give standing cards their own liveness semantics.",
"3e0c3cf7": "The progress patrol sends you-filed-nothing nags after work was durably recorded; it accused the relief owner four times in one day with the filings sitting right there. Recon why, then fix it.",
"0c1ad73a": "What happened on X since Monday currently needs a mind to assemble from raw rows. Add a deterministic progress-since query that returns dated factual rows for a window and scope, with zero inference baked in.",
"bcb85145": "A hung-work watchdog that detects deterministically from durable facts first, and only then applies bounded, evidence-labeled inference, instead of putting judgment calls in the substrate.",
"9e5684ae": "When a fence arms or a credential goes stale, the owner finds out only through per-turn errors later. Post one substrate-authored line into the owner stream at arm time and surface it in doctor and readiness.",
"b8802849": "The CLI finds its session credential by walking up from the current directory, so a session running in the wrong workdir silently acts AS ITS PARENT (observed live). Spawned sessions need isolated identity.",
"9970877d": "Anyone who reads the CLI token from gateway.json can POST straight to the gateway with asUser=mike and forge the human's identity, bypassing the entire CLI accountability layer. The most critical security item on the slate.",
"893b0503": "A review verdict was fabricated in wake delivery, and content apparently leaked between tenants. Judgment rows must be unforgeable and content must never cross tenants.",
"609e19b9": "An artifact can be state RELEASED with no retrievable content: NULL hash, origin workdir cleaned, and no verb to fetch released bytes. Released must mean the content survives.",
"62f23012": "artifact-record stores whatever sha256 the caller claims and never recomputes it; a recorded digest was proven to mismatch the actual file. Compute digests from the referenced bytes.",
"132b4d8b": "Attest notes over 2,000 characters are silently truncated, so a long forensic walkthrough reads as complete with its tail gone. Reject or expose truncation; never silently alter durable evidence.",
"ce728dff": "A verdict can be filed with NO note at all (blank is rejected but missing passes), so a zero-reasoning rubber stamp is representable, and one was filed by accident. A judgment row must carry its reasoning.",
"a55a4ce7": "Commit references point at per-session disposable clones, so once the workdir dies, merge state becomes unknowable even though the commit survived; five ATC items render gray rings for exactly this. Reference commits against durable repos.",
"9fae0bd5": "A sha-referenced ruling stopped resolving because its source workdir was deleted, and the reader could not even detect the dangle. Spec refs must persist durably and dangle detectably; this slate itself nearly suffered this before landing in tightbeam-specs.",
"cf28d526": "Structured commitRefs are refused on review-ready evidence (allowed only at completion), while coder guidance requires exactly that ref before review, so refs end up as prose in notes. Accept them where the process needs them.",
"8da6cf3e": "A witness in one product cannot attach first-hand evidence to another product's incident card; the artifact-record is refused and the evidence orphans onto the reporter's own item. Let evidence land on the owning card.",
}
